/T (She wasn't trying to stand out. She wasn't surrounded by friends, and she wasn't laughing loudly or looking around the room. She simply walked in, carrying a notebook against her chest, quietly searching for an empty seat. The morning sunlight slipped through the high windows, catching the edge of her neatly tied hair for just a moment before she disappeared into the crowd. Jo watched her sit down three rows ahead. That was all. There was no dramatic music, no slow motion, no fireworks. Just an ordinary moment that somehow stayed with him. He didn't know her name. He didn't know which class she belonged to. He didn't even know whether she had noticed him looking at her. But for the rest of the afternoon, he found himself remembering that moment. Not her face exactly. Not even the way she looked. Just the feeling of having noticed someone for the first time. And, somehow, that felt important.)
