/T (The following Monday, Jo stood outside the music room with his guitar case hanging from his shoulder. The annual cultural programme was only a few days away, and the school was beginning to feel unusually busy. Students rehearsed in every available space, while music, laughter and nervous conversations filled the corridors. "You look nervous," Arjun said. Jo glanced at him. "I'm not." "You've been staring at that guitar for five minutes." Jo smiled. "Maybe I'm thinking." "About?" Before Jo could answer, the music room door opened. "Jo?" the teacher called. "You're next." He took a breath and walked inside. The rehearsal wasn't perfect. He missed a chord halfway through the song and had to start the section again. Daniel, watching from the back, whispered, "Professional." Jo gave him a warning look. The teacher laughed. "Again, Jo." This time, he played it perfectly. When the final chord faded, she nodded. "Much better." Jo smiled. Maybe he had never been afraid of performing. Maybe he had simply been afraid of wanting something to go well.)
