/T (The apartment was on the third floor of a building that looked like it had been painted several times and cleaned only once. Jo stood outside the door, checking the number on his phone for the third time. "Please tell me this is the right place." The door opened before he could knock again. A boy about his age looked at him. "You Jo?" "Yeah." "Come in, bro." Jo stepped inside. The apartment was smaller than the photographs had suggested. A three-bedroom flat. Six people. Two mattresses already occupied the living room. Shoes near the entrance. Clothes hanging from chairs. A half-empty packet of chips on the dining table. And somewhere in the background, someone was arguing about who had finished the milk. Jo smiled. So this was Bengaluru. He put his bags down and looked around. It wasn't home. Not yet. But perhaps home didn't always begin that way. Sometimes... it began with six strangers sharing a three-bedroom apartment and figuring out how to survive together.)
