/T (By evening, Jo had met all five of his roommates. There was Aravind, who seemed permanently attached to his headphones; Sameer, who could turn any conversation into a debate; and Rahul, who had already claimed half the refrigerator as his personal property. The other two were more interested in welcoming Jo with questions. "Do you cook?" "A little." "Good. We need someone who can cook." Jo laughed. "I said a little." "That's enough." Someone from the kitchen shouted, "Who wants coffee?" Three people answered at once. Jo shook his head, smiling. Within an hour, the apartment no longer felt quite as unfamiliar. It was noisy, messy and completely different from home. But there was something comforting about it. Maybe college life wasn't going to be so bad after all. That night, Jo unpacked his guitar and placed it beside his mattress. One of his roommates looked over. "You play?" Jo smiled. "Yeah." "Sing too?" "Sometimes." The room went quiet for a second. Then someone grinned. "Bro, you're definitely performing at the next apartment party." Jo laughed. "Let's survive tomorrow first.")
