/T (Years later, Jo would still keep the diary. It sat safely on a shelf, its pages worn from years of being opened and read again. Sometimes Mouna would take it down and laugh at his old handwriting. Sometimes Jo would find a forgotten note tucked between the pages. Neither of them ever threw it away. It had started as a simple way to communicate. Somehow, it had become a record of everything they had built together. Jo looked at Mouna. "Do you remember what you wrote the first time?" She smiled. She knew exactly what he meant. Mouna. Just her name. The first word of a story neither of them knew they were beginning. Jo smiled. Some stories are written with words. Theirs had been written with silence)
/E (Years later, Jo would still keep the diary. It sat safely on a shelf, its pages worn from years of being opened and read again. Sometimes Mouna would take it down and laugh at his old handwriting. Sometimes Jo would find a forgotten note tucked between the pages. Neither of them ever threw it away. It had started as a simple way to communicate. Somehow, it had become a record of everything they had built together. Jo looked at Mouna. "Do you remember what you wrote the first time?" She smiled. She knew exactly what he meant. Mouna. Just her name. The first word of a story neither of them knew they were beginning. Jo smiled. Some stories are written with words. Theirs had been written with silence)
/Pg 152 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 773 0 R
/T (That evening, Jo and Mouna sat together in the quiet of their home. The diary rested between them. Jo opened it to a blank page. For a moment, he thought about everything they had written before. Then he picked up the pen. He wrote: I think I finally understand. Mouna looked at him. He continued: Love was never about finding someone who could hear every word. He turned the diary toward her. It was about finding someone who could understand the things you couldn't say. Mouna smiled. She took the pen and wrote beneath it: And staying long enough to learn. Jo smiled. He closed the diary. Outside, the world was still noisy. But between them, there was a silence that no longer needed to be explained. It was simply theirs. Theirs was a silent love.)
/E (That evening, Jo and Mouna sat together in the quiet of their home. The diary rested between them. Jo opened it to a blank page. For a moment, he thought about everything they had written before. Then he picked up the pen. He wrote: I think I finally understand. Mouna looked at him. He continued: Love was never about finding someone who could hear every word. He turned the diary toward her. It was about finding someone who could understand the things you couldn't say. Mouna smiled. She took the pen and wrote beneath it: And staying long enough to learn. Jo smiled. He closed the diary. Outside, the world was still noisy. But between them, there was a silence that no longer needed to be explained. It was simply theirs. Theirs was a silent love.)
/Pg 153 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 774 0 R
/T (Years had passed, but some things never changed. Mouna still kept the diary. Jo still played his guitar. They still went to church together on Sundays whenever they could. And sometimes, without saying anything, they would simply sit beside each other. Jo would look at her. Mouna would look back. A smile was enough. Because after everything they had learned, they no longer needed to explain every feeling. They had learned each other's language. They had learned each other's silence. And somewhere between the words they wrote and the words they never needed to say, they had built a life together. A quiet life. A beautiful one. Their silent love.)
/E (Years had passed, but some things never changed. Mouna still kept the diary. Jo still played his guitar. They still went to church together on Sundays whenever they could. And sometimes, without saying anything, they would simply sit beside each other. Jo would look at her. Mouna would look back. A smile was enough. Because after everything they had learned, they no longer needed to explain every feeling. They had learned each other's language. They had learned each other's silence. And somewhere between the words they wrote and the words they never needed to say, they had built a life together. A quiet life. A beautiful one. Their silent love.)
/Pg 154 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 775 0 R
/T (Years had passed since that Sunday. Life had become fuller and busier in ways neither of them could have imagined. There were responsibilities, work, ordinary problems and ordinary joys. But some things remained unchanged. On Sundays, whenever they could, Jo and Mouna still went to church together. Sometimes they talked. Sometimes they simply walked beside each other. And sometimes, they sat quietly in the same place where so much of their story had begun. Jo would look at her and smile. Mouna would smile back. Nothing more was needed. They had learned that love did not have to be loud to be certain. Sometimes, it was simply choosing the same person, again and again, every day.)
/E (Years had passed since that Sunday. Life had become fuller and busier in ways neither of them could have imagined. There were responsibilities, work, ordinary problems and ordinary joys. But some things remained unchanged. On Sundays, whenever they could, Jo and Mouna still went to church together. Sometimes they talked. Sometimes they simply walked beside each other. And sometimes, they sat quietly in the same place where so much of their story had begun. Jo would look at her and smile. Mouna would smile back. Nothing more was needed. They had learned that love did not have to be loud to be certain. Sometimes, it was simply choosing the same person, again and again, every day.)
/Pg 155 0 R
/K [0]
